# "necessary" keeps one sense wherever it appears. In S7 it means not possible not to be, that is, not subject to generation and corruption.
# S8–S10 use the same sense when they speak of necessary things whose necessity is caused by another (incorruptible but caused beings). The text never switches to logical necessity, so no bridge is named.
# Unsure about once_nothing. S4 says nothing "could have been" in existence, but A1/C1 and S5–S6 read it as nothing "was" in existence.
# That is a weakening from possibility to fact, not a word taking two senses, so I left it as one.
# Similarly, the move from "each contingent thing at some time is not" (P3) to "at one time nothing was" is a change in quantifier scope, not a change in what a word means.
